\description{
Computes the distribution of the total number of contacts of a media plan
whose vehicles are linked by a Gaussian copula. Each vehicle has either a
bounded number of insertions (Beta-Binomial marginal, \code{"bbd"}) or an
unbounded stream of impressions (Negative Binomial marginal, \code{"nbd"}).
\code{distrib_gcbbd()} is the special case in which every vehicle is \code{"bbd"}.
}
\details{
\strong{Marginals.} For a \code{"bbd"} vehicle the number of contacts is
\code{X | P ~ Binomial(n, P)} with \code{P ~ Beta(alpha, beta)}; \code{alpha + beta} is
obtained exactly from \code{r1} and \code{r2}, because the probability of being
exposed to two insertions is \code{2 * r1 - r2 = E[P^2]}. For an \code{"nbd"} vehicle
the number of impressions is \code{X | L ~ Poisson(L)} with \code{L} Gamma distributed
over \code{n} unit blocks; the shape and the mean per block are obtained by
solving exactly the two equations \code{r1 = 1 - (1 + s / r)^(-r)} and
\verb{r2 = 1 - (1 + 2 s / r)^(-r)}. Hence, for both families, the reach of one
and of two unit blocks is reproduced exactly, and the expected number of
contacts is \code{n * r1} for \code{"bbd"} and \code{n * s} for \code{"nbd"} (where \code{s >= r1}
because one block can deliver several impressions to the same person).
Contacts are therefore exposures for \code{"bbd"} vehicles and impressions for
\code{"nbd"} vehicles, and the gross rating points (GRPs) are \code{100} times the
expected number of contacts.

\strong{Dependence.} Given a latent Gaussian vector with correlation matrix
\code{Sigma}, vehicles are conditionally independent; the latent quantity of each
vehicle is mapped to its propensity to be exposed to one unit block
(\code{P} for \code{"bbd"}, \code{1 - exp(-L / n)} for \code{"nbd"}). The duplication of the
first unit blocks of vehicles \code{i} and \code{j} is then
\code{D_ij = E[pi_i * pi_j]}, which is a strictly increasing function of
\code{Sigma[i, j]}. Each off-diagonal element is calibrated, one pair at a time,
by solving \code{D_ij(Sigma[i, j]) = target} with quadrature on a normal grid.
Pairwise calibration does not guarantee a positive definite \code{Sigma} when
there are three or more vehicles; if it is not, the eigenvalues are floored
and a warning is issued.

\strong{Integration.} The distribution is the average, over a deterministic
antithetic Halton sample of the latent vector (moment-matched to a unit
covariance), of the exact convolution of the conditional marginals. No random
number generator is used, so results are reproducible and the global random
state is untouched. Increase \code{n_qmc} to assess the numerical error, and
check \code{diagnostics}, which reports the error of the fitted reach of one
block and of the fitted duplications. With more than six vehicles a larger
\code{n_qmc} is advisable; at most 12 vehicles are supported.

\strong{Truncation.} If any vehicle is \code{"nbd"}, the support is unbounded. The
distribution is reported for \verb{0, ..., k_max}, and the last category is
"\code{k_max} or more" (\code{open_last_bin = TRUE}). The default \code{k_max} is the sum of
the number of insertions of the \code{"bbd"} vehicles and of the \code{1 - 1e-6}
quantiles of the \code{"nbd"} vehicles. For a plan with only \code{"bbd"} vehicles the
support is \verb{0, ..., sum(insertions)} and is exact unless a smaller \code{k_max}
is requested.

\strong{Scale.} Probabilities are entered and returned in the unit given by
\code{scale}. The scale is never guessed: with \code{scale = "proportion"} any value
above one is an error.

\strong{Limitations.} The reach of one and of two insertions of each vehicle is
reproduced exactly, and the expected number of contacts follows from them.
The duplication of the first insertions is reproduced only approximately,
because the correlations are calibrated one pair at a time; when the
calibrated matrix is not positive definite it is adjusted, and the size of the
deviation is reported in \code{diagnostics$dup_error}. This happens more often as
the number of vehicles grows and when the duplications come from observed
data. The reach of one and two insertions and the duplication do not
determine the distribution of contacts: the shape of the distribution beyond
them comes from the assumptions of the model (Beta or Gamma propensities, a
Gaussian copula, and exchangeable insertions within a vehicle), and models
with the same inputs and other assumptions give other distributions. In
comparisons with other multivehicle models on survey-derived data the
differences in accuracy were small and not stable out of sample, so the
choice of model should rest on its assumptions and on the diagnostics, not on
a presumption of higher accuracy.
}
